<p>In 1903, Gilbert Woglom of New York composed <a href="https://archive.org/details/the-strand/The%20Strand%20v26%201903/page/274/mode/2up">&#8220;The Tramp&#8217;s Gratitude,&#8221;</a> a musical composition in which the names of the notes, taken successively, spell out a poem:</p>
<p>A bad-faced, faded, aged cad<br />
Begged a feed, a bed, bedad.<br />
Bedded, fed, a café added,<br />
Bed, bag, baggage, egad, cad cabbaged.</p>
<p>The longest English word that can be spelled with musical note names alone is CABBAGE-FACED.</p>
